What ZangbandTK has done to these tilesets
==========================================

All four sets came from Angband and none of them was drawn for this game, so
none of them had art for anything ZangbandTK adds.  Rather than leave the
imported bestiary and the whole wilderness rendering as ASCII letters among the
pictures, we have changed them.  This file says exactly how, per tileset, so
that anybody -- a packager, a tileset author, somebody diffing against upstream
Angband -- can see what is ours without reading the pixels.

Two kinds of change, and the difference matters
-----------------------------------------------

**A mapping** adds a `.prf` file saying "this thing looks like that cell".  It
adds no art and alters no image: the picture was always in the sheet, and
nothing but a lookup table has changed.

**A modification** writes pixels into the sheet.

Both are recorded below.  The distinction is not pedantry -- it is the whole of
whether a licence that forbids modification has been respected.


Per tileset
-----------

**gervais (32x32) -- mapped only.  The image is untouched.**

  Angband maps 1151 of this sheet's 3840 cells.  Another 1239 unmapped cells
  hold art, and a contiguous block of it -- sheet rows 23 to 25 -- is a
  wilderness terrain set that Angband never had a use for.  `feat-ztk.prf`
  points thirteen ZangbandTK features at cells that were already there.

  `32x32.png` is byte-identical to Angband's.

**adam-bolt (16x16), nomad (16x16), old (8x8) -- modified.**

  These had no wilderness art to map, so it was generated:
  `scripts/tiles/make-terrain.py` appends **one new row of thirty tiles** to the
  bottom of each sheet -- ten terrain features in three lighting variants -- and
  writes the matching `feat-ztk.prf`.

  Nothing already in those sheets is touched.  The row is added below the
  existing art, so every original cell keeps its coordinates and every original
  pixel its value.  The colours are sampled from the sheet being extended, so
  the new tiles are in that tileset's own palette rather than in one we chose.

  The script is deterministic and idempotent: re-running it reproduces the same
  bytes, and reclaims the row it wrote last time rather than adding another.

**All four -- mapped, for the monsters.**

  `graf-ztk.prf` in each directory gives the 234 imported monsters that resemble
  an Angband monster the picture of that monster, so they are not letters in a
  tiled game.  Generated by `scripts/tiles/borrow-tiles.py`.  Uniques are
  deliberately left as text: a borrowed portrait on a named character says
  something false, and Oberon of Amber came out wearing the Mouth of Sauron's
  face.

  Each tileset's own `graf-*.prf` gains `%:` lines loading the above.  That is a
  modification of a text file, not of any art.


May we?
-------

  * **Adam Bolt's** may be "redistributed and used for any purpose, with or
    without modification".  Explicit.
  * **David Gervais'** is Creative Commons Attribution 3.0, which permits
    modification with attribution.  We have not modified it in any case.
  * **The original 8x8 and Nomad's** carry no licence statement, here or in
    Angband's own `copying.rst`.  They are not listed among the exceptions in
    LICENSE.md, so they stand on the project licence like the rest of the tree.
    That is a reading of a silence rather than a permission, and it is recorded
    as such rather than assumed.
  * **Shockbolt's** was removed in 3.95.0 and is not here.  Its licence grants
    use with Angband and withholds it for "other games or projects", which is
    what this is.

See LICENSE.md and docs/copying.rst for the full terms.


What is still missing
---------------------

Not everything has a tile yet, and the gaps are deliberate rather than
forgotten.  Gervais has no inn, magesmith, chaos tower or recharger; the three
generated sets have none of those, nor a dungeon mouth or a secret door.  Those
are buildings and openings rather than ground textures -- shapes, not rules --
and a borrowed picture for one of them would be actively wrong rather than
merely absent.  They render as ASCII until somebody draws them.
